\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Here is an analysis of the requested directions, drawing on prior art from incremental SAT, constraint logic programming, and incremental build systems.\
Directions (a) and (e) have been merged, as computing intersections over requirement intervals is the practical application of building a lattice algebra over version space. Similarly, (c) and (f) have been merged, as the monotonic properties of boolean formulas are the fundamental mathematical reason why incremental SAT clause reuse works.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf2 \strokec2 (a) & (e) Merged: Semver-Aware Fact Lifting & Lattice Algebras\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf2 \strokec2 Concept:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  If a requirement edit does not intersect the 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 VersionSet
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2  region a derived fact specifically covers, the fact remains valid because the modified constraint space is completely disjoint from the conflict space. This requires formalizing requirement changes as lattice operations.\

\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 1. Prior Art:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 {\listtext	\'95	}PubGrub / 
\f3 \cf2 \strokec2 resolvo
\f1 \cf2 \strokec2 :
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  PubGrub's core algorithm (Nelson, 2020) explicitly relies on interval algebra (unions and intersections of version ranges) to compute derivations.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 {\listtext	\'95	}Abstract Interpretation:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  Cousot & Cousot (1977). The concept of tracking validity as an interval in "edit space" maps to lattice intervals.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 {\listtext	\'95	}OPAM / Dose3:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  Abate et al. (2012) "Dependency solving: a separate concern in component-based systems" explicitly models version dependencies as constraints and uses interval sets.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 2. Strongest Candidate Technique:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  
\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 Disjoint Edit Bypassing.
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  When a dependency row changes from V_\{old\} to V_\{new\}, compute the symmetric difference of their requirement intervals for each modified edge: \\Delta = (Req_\{new\} \\cup Req_\{old\}) \\setminus (Req_\{new\} \\cap Req_\{old\}). When assessing a previously learned fact with a known region R_\{fact\}, if R_\{fact\} \\cap \\Delta = \\emptyset, the fact is strictly preserved. The edit occurred entirely outside the region where the fact's proof operates.\

\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 3. Soundness Conditions:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf2 \strokec2 {\listtext	\'95	}De Morgan operations on 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 VersionSet
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2  must be exhaustive and perfectly sound (no off-by-one errors on half-open boundaries).\
{\listtext	\'95	}The fact's 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 support
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2  must not implicitly depend on the 
\f4\i \cf2 \strokec2 absence
\f0\i0 \cf2 \strokec2  of versions within \\Delta. (If the proof DAG relies on a negative hypothesis\'97"version X does not exist"\'97and the edit introduces version X inside the fact's region, the fact breaks).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 4. Falsifier:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  The 
\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 req-incompatible-bump
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  (e.g., 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 async-graphql 0.9.4 -> 0.10.0: ^0.9.4 -> ^0.10.0
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2 ). The \\Delta here is massive. If a fact was derived asserting that a conflict happens 
\f4\i \cf2 \strokec2 below
\f0\i0 \cf2 \strokec2  
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 0.10.0
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2 , the edit directly intersects the upper bound of the old requirement. The intersection R_\{fact\} \\cap \\Delta will almost always be non-empty, meaning this technique degrades to standard invalidation for the most common real-world churn.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf2 \strokec2 (b) Per-Edge Validity Instead of Per-Row\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf2 \strokec2 Concept:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  Facts should be indexed by the specific dependency edges their 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 support
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2  set touched, not the manifest row. This directly exploits the 7.29x fineness ratio.\

\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 1. Prior Art:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 {\listtext	\'95	}Incremental Datalog / Differential Dataflow:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  McSherry et al. (2013) and DDlog (Ryzhyk et al., 2019). These systems track provenance at the granularity of individual tuples (edges), propagating invalidations only to views (facts) directly derived from modified inputs.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 {\listtext	\'95	}Salsa (Rust):
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  An incremental computation framework based on adapting memoized queries to track fine-grained dependencies.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 2. Strongest Candidate Technique:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  
\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 Bipartite Edge-Fact Indexing.
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  Decompose the manifest row into unique 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 EdgeID
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2 s (e.g., 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 Hash(PkgIx, Target, DepName)
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2 ). A learned fact's 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 support
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2  set stores a list of 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 EdgeID
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2 s and their exact observed hashes, not 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 snapshot_id
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2 s of the whole row. When a package bumps V_1 \\to V_2, diff the manifest to find which 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 EdgeID
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2 s changed. Only drop facts whose 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 support
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2  contains a modified 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 EdgeID
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2 .\

\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 3. Soundness Conditions:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 {\listtext	\'95	}Exhaustive Support Recording:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  The classic soundness bug in incremental systems is an under-recorded dependency. If the solver evaluates 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 features2
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2  or an 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 optional
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2  flag to make a routing decision but doesn't record that specific field's 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 EdgeID
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2  in the support set, the fact will incorrectly survive a relevant change.\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 {\listtext	\'95	}Absence Tracking:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  If an edge is 
\f4\i \cf2 \strokec2 not
\f0\i0 \cf2 \strokec2  present, and the solver uses that absence to prune a search path, the "absence of 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 EdgeID
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2 " must be recorded as a pseudo-edge in the support set.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 4. Falsifier:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  The 
\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 dep-removed
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  case (e.g., 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 async-graphql 1.10.12 -> 1.11.0: deps[graphql-parser]: removed
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2 ). If the solver does not explicitly encode "Edge X exists" in the 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 support
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2  set, and merely tracks the properties of Edge X, removing the edge entirely might orphan the fact. The resolver must treat edge-removal as a destructive update to the 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 EdgeID
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2  state, invalidating all downstream facts.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf2 \strokec2 (c) & (f) Merged: Monotonicity & Incremental-SAT Clause Reuse\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf2 \strokec2 Concept:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  In boolean logic, adding constraints to a formula preserves the UNSAT nature of any previously derived resolvent. 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 req-narrowed
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2  and 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 dep-added (required)
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2  strictly add constraints.\

\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 1. Prior Art:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 {\listtext	\'95	}Assumption-Based Incremental SAT:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  E\'e9n & S\'f6rensson (2003) "An Extensible SAT-solver" (MiniSat). This is the foundational paper for solving under assumptions (push/pop).\
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 {\listtext	\'95	}Monotonicity in First-Order Logic:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  "Adding a clause to a CNF formula preserves UNSAT" is a fundamental property of classical monotonic logic (Entailment monotonicity).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 2. Strongest Candidate Technique:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  
\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 One-Sided Edit Preservation.
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  Categorize manifest edits during the version bump.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf2 \strokec2 {\listtext	\'95	}If Req_\{new\} \\subset Req_\{old\} (
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 req-narrowed
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2 ), this is constraint tightening.\
{\listtext	\'95	}If a required dependency is added, this is constraint tightening. If the diff between V_1 and V_2 consists 
\f4\i \cf2 \strokec2 exclusively
\f0\i0 \cf2 \strokec2  of constraint tightening moves, all facts derived at V_1 are unconditionally valid at V_2 and require no re-derivation.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 3. Soundness Conditions:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf2 \strokec2 {\listtext	\'95	}The system must not possess any non-monotonic deduction rules. For example, if Rodin has heuristic fallback rules (e.g., "if X is constrained, assume Y"), tightening X might alter Y in ways that break the proof DAG.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 {\listtext	\'95	}Feature Flags:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  Adding an optional dependency (
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 dep-added (optional)
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2 ) is 
\f4\i \cf2 \strokec2 not
\f0\i0 \cf2 \strokec2  strictly a tightening move if it introduces new boolean variables (the feature literals) that open new satisfiability paths in the hyper-rectangle.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 4. Falsifier:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  The 
\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 feature-map co-occurring
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  breaks (134 instances in the taxonomy). A 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 req-narrowed
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2  change that is accompanied by a new feature flag routing means the overall boolean space is no longer a strict subset of the old space. The new feature introduces a new dimension to the 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 Region
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2  hyper-rectangle. A fact mapped to the old space cannot soundly assert it holds in the newly expanded dimensional space without extending its proof DAG.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf2 \strokec2 (d) Fact Weakening Instead of Invalidation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf2 \strokec2 Concept:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  Instead of invalidating a fact when an edge widens, intersect the fact's region with the new constraint space to salvage a narrower, still-true conflict region.\

\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 1. Prior Art:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 {\listtext	\'95	}ATMS (Assumption-based Truth Maintenance Systems):
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  de Kleer (1986). ATMS nodes retain multiple minimal environments (regions) justifying a fact; if one environment is invalidated, others may survive.\
\ls8\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 {\listtext	\'95	}Clause Vivification / Shrinking:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  Piette et al. (2008). Techniques in CDCL that attempt to shrink learned clauses to make them more general or salvage them after state changes.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 2. Strongest Candidate Technique:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  
\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 Proof-DAG Intersection Scaling.
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  When a 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 req-widened
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2  edit occurs on edge E, locate all facts supported by E. Instead of deleting the fact, apply Req_\{new\} \\cap Req_\{old\} (which for widening is just Req_\{old\}). The fact's internal region R is scaled down by applying this intersection to its E-axis. The proof DAG is retained, but its terminal hypothesis is stamped with the narrower region.\

\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 3. Soundness Conditions:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf2 \strokec2 {\listtext	\'95	}The CDCL engine must natively support subsumption and localized weakening without requiring a full replay of the proof DAG from the root. If Rodin requires a byte-for-byte replay of premises, modifying the terminal region necessitates mutating the historical hypotheses inside the DAG.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 4. Falsifier:
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  The 
\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 req-reshaped
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  break (e.g., 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 openssl 0.7.6 -> 0.7.7: >= 0.2, < 0.4 -> ^0.4
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2 ). If the weakening logic is syntactic, it will fail to recognize that the sets are identical and may erroneously weaken or drop the fact. More critically, the 417 occurrences of 
\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 req-narrowed
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  (which shift floors) result in Req_\{new\} \\cap Req_\{old\} being vastly smaller than the original space. The computational overhead of intersecting and rewriting the proof DAGs is likely higher than simply re-deriving the conflict on the smaller space.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf2 \strokec2 Ranking by Soundness-per-Engineering-Cost\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf2 \strokec2 {\listtext	1.	}(b) Per-edge validity (Highest ROI):
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  The taxonomy data shows a 
\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 7.29x
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  multiplier in stability by just indexing by edge. The engineering cost is moderate (rewriting the 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 support
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2  set to track 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 EdgeID
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2  hashes instead of row snapshots), but it avoids complex interval math and safely handles all cases, including complex lockstep sibling bumps (e.g., 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 async-graphql
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2 ).\
\ls10\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 {\listtext	2.	}(c)/(f) Monotonicity / One-sided edits (High ROI, Low Cost):
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  Safely ignoring invalidations for the ~79% of real breaks that are purely 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 req-narrowed
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2  or 
\f2 \cf2 \strokec2 dep-added (required)
\f0 \cf2 \strokec2  requires very little math. You just classify the diff and skip the flush. It stacks perfectly with (b).\
\ls10\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 {\listtext	3.	}(a)/(e) Semver-aware fact lifting (Moderate ROI, High Cost):
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  Implementing rigorous De Morgan interval diffs to check disjointness is computationally cheap at runtime, but ensuring there are no soundness holes in the edge-cases of semver parsers is notoriously difficult. Its actual hit rate is likely low due to most changes being shifts, not disjoint jumps.\
\ls10\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \strokec2 {\listtext	4.	}(d) Fact weakening (Negative ROI):
\f0\b0 \cf2 \strokec2  Rewriting proof DAGs to accommodate shrunken regions introduces immense complexity for minimal gain. In SAT, clause minimization is generally done 
\f4\i \cf2 \strokec2 during
\f0\i0 \cf2 \strokec2  conflict analysis, not retrospectively after formula edits, because the overhead of tracking the exact dimensional intersection of a historical proof is worse than just re-deriving it.\
}